Monoclonal Gammopathy of Undetermined Significance (MGUS)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Serum M-protein <3 g/dL | Bone marrow clonal plasma cells <10% | Complete absence of CRAB criteria (no end-organ damage)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-008
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal panic attack related to incidental laboratory findings of a blood protein abnormality, requiring urgent clinical evaluation to exclude overt malignancy complications.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required due to severe acute anxiety, palpitations, and somatic distress triggered by accessing an uninterpreted laboratory report online. Immediate clinical review was necessary to exclude acute hypercalcemic crisis, impending spinal cord compression, or severe anemia, preventing further psychological deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I saw my blood results online, it says I have a monoclonal protein, and my chest hurts from panicking that my bones are going to snap.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with profound acute health anxiety following an incidental finding on routine blood testing. Upon detailed questioning, there is a complete absence of bone pain or skeletal lesions. The patient reports no constitutional symptoms such as profound weight loss, night sweats, or unexpected fatigue. There is a lack of recurrent severe bacterial infections. The patient admits to an unrelated persistent subacute cough following a prior viral respiratory infection three weeks ago.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe health anxiety leading to compulsive internet research and insomnia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/82 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient appears visibly anxious, clutching printed lab reports, but is in no physical distress.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive physical examination reveals normal findings without hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy. Neurological exam reveals mild incidental paresthesia in the distal extremities, which is stable. Spinal palpation elicits zero point-tenderness. Respiratory examination is completely clear, and a recent outpatient normal chest radiograph effectively rules out acute pulmonary pathology despite the subacute cough.
Environmental Observations: A computer desk cluttered with printed medical articles about Multiple Myeloma.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Monoclonal Gammopathy of Undetermined Significance (MGUS) - Status: Asymptomatic clinically, complicated by acute severe health anxiety.
Differentials:

Multiple Myeloma - Ruled out clinically due to the complete absence of bone pain, preservation of normal renal function, normal baseline hemoglobin levels, and absence of hypercalcemia symptoms (CRAB criteria negative).

Waldenström Macroglobulinemia - Ruled out due to the absence of hyperviscosity symptoms (no visual changes, bleeding) and lack of hepatosplenomegaly.

Acute Hypercalcemia - Ruled out due to normal mental status, absence of polyuria, and normal bowel habits.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This home assessment was required after hours due to the patient experiencing a severe acute panic response to an uninterpreted, complex laboratory result. Telephone triage correctly identified the need for immediate clinical correlation to exclude the acute, life-threatening sequelae of plasma cell dyscrasias, such as hypercalcemia or spinal cord compression. A comprehensive physical assessment firmly established the absence of end-organ damage or constitutional decline. By definitively translating the clinical data and ruling out acute malignancy complications, this intervention immediately terminated the acute panic cycle, avoiding an inappropriate emergency department presentation for a chronic, asymptomatic condition.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Extensive clinical education and reassurance provided. The expected investigation results include a serum monoclonal protein concentration less than 3 g/dL with less than ten percent clonal plasma cells in the bone marrow, which will assist in confirming the benign diagnosis during the planned hematology follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: To break the cycle of health anxiety, immediately block or disconnect your internet router for the remainder of the night; removing access to medical search engines will drastically reduce catastrophic thinking and allow your nervous system to return to baseline.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to organize a non-urgent hematology consultation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops sudden, severe, unremitting back pain, sudden weakness in the legs, or an inability to urinate.

